.TH MMAP 2 "July 18, 2026"
.SH NAME
mmap, munmap, mprotect, msync, madvise, mincore, mlock, munlock \- mapped memory operations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>
.PP
void *mmap(void *addr, size_t len, int prot, int flags,
    int fd, off_t offset);
int munmap(void *addr, size_t len);
int mprotect(void *addr, size_t len, int prot);
int msync(void *addr, size_t len, int flags);
int madvise(void *addr, size_t len, int advice);
int mincore(void *addr, size_t len, unsigned char *vec);
int mlock(const void *addr, size_t len);
int munlock(const void *addr, size_t len);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mmap
creates a mapping in the calling process.
The protection is a combination of
.BR PROT_READ ,
.BR PROT_WRITE ,
and
.BR PROT_EXEC ,
or is
.BR PROT_NONE .
Exactly one of
.B MAP_PRIVATE
and
.B MAP_SHARED
must be supplied.
.PP
The current implementation supports demand-zero anonymous mappings with
.B MAP_ANON
and regular-file mappings with either
.B MAP_PRIVATE
or
.BR MAP_SHARED .
For anonymous mappings
.I fd
must be -1 and
.I offset
must be zero.
Mapping
.B /dev/zero
at offset zero has the same demand-zero behavior and may be private or shared.
Descriptors returned by
.IR shm_open (2)
map resizable kernel-resident shared objects without a filesystem backing
file.
.B MAP_PRIVATE
file mappings isolate writes with copy-on-write.
.B MAP_SHARED
file mappings share resident pages with other mappings and coherent buffered
file I/O; dirty pages are written by
.IR msync (2)
or normal filesystem synchronization.
.PP
Character devices may support controlled
.B MAP_SHARED
mappings through their driver.
Such mappings cannot be executable, require descriptor permissions matching
the requested protections, and expose only the physical range and cache mode
approved by the driver.
The address is a hint unless
.B MAP_FIXED
is present.  A page-aligned fixed mapping atomically replaces mappings in its
range after all arguments and required VM resources have been validated.
File and device offsets must be page aligned.
.PP
.I Munmap
removes all mappings in the page-aligned range.
.I Mprotect
changes protection after verifying that the complete range is mapped.
Lengths are rounded up to the 4096-byte VM page size.
.PP
For anonymous and device mappings,
.I msync
validates the range and completes without I/O.
For shared regular-file mappings it writes dirty pages and, with
.BR MS_INVALIDATE ,
drops clean resident pages so a later access faults them in again.
.I Madvise
validates its range and advice; advice is currently informational.
.I Mincore
stores one byte per VM page in
.I vec
and sets
.B MINCORE_INCORE
when an object page is resident; controlled device mappings are always
resident.
.I Mlock
makes all pages in a mapped range resident and ineligible for pageout until
.I munlock
is called or the range is unmapped.  Locks are not inherited across
.IR fork (2).
.SH RETURN VALUES
.I Mmap
returns the mapping address on success and
.B MAP_FAILED
on failure.
The other calls return zero on success and -1 on failure.
.SH ERRORS
These calls may fail with
.BR EINVAL
for invalid flags, protection, alignment, length, offset, or advice;
.BR EFAULT
for an invalid user vector or an unmapped range;
.BR ENOMEM
when no address range or VM resource is available;
.BR EACCES
when descriptor access does not permit the requested shared protection;
.BR ENODEV
when a file type or character-device mapping is unsupported;
.BR EOVERFLOW
when a file range is not representable; or
.BR EOPNOTSUPP
for an operation not implemented by the selected mapping type.
.SH SEE ALSO
brk(2), fork(2), getpagesize(2), shm_open(2)
